      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "We compared the effects of intrathecal administration of antiserum against calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) between thermo- and mechano-nociceptive responses, using experimental hyperalgesic rats. An intrathecal administration of anti-CGRP antiserum, but not antiserum absorbed by synthetic CGRP, normalized either adjuvant- or carrageenin-induced hyperalgesia both in the paw radiant heat and the paw pressure tests, with little effect on non-hyperalgesic paws. These results suggest that endogenous CGRP, probably present in primary afferents, promotes both thermo- and mechano-nociceptive transmission in the spinal dorsal horn, at least in the hyperalgesic states with inflammations."},
